## 🔧 Step 1 — Environment Setup

### 1.1 Enable TCP/IP on ALL Servers

> ⚠️ TCP/IP is **mandatory** for database mirroring. SQL Server sends transaction log records over the network and only supports TCP/IP for this.

Verify TCP/IP listeners are active:

```sql
SELECT * FROM sys.dm_tcp_listener_states;
```

> ❌ If this returns `NULL` — TCP/IP is not enabled. Go to **SQL Server Configuration Manager** → **SQL Server Network Configuration** → Enable **TCP/IP**.

---

## 💾 Step 2 — Backup Databases

Run these on the **Principal Server**.

> 📝 Copy-only backups work too, but regular backups are fine.

### Full Backup
```sql
BACKUP DATABASE [YourDatabaseName]
TO DISK = N'C:\Backups\YourDatabaseName_FULL.bak'
WITH FORMAT, INIT, NAME = N'Full Backup', STATS = 10;
```

### Log Backup
```sql
BACKUP LOG [YourDatabaseName]
TO DISK = N'C:\Backups\YourDatabaseName_LOG.bak'
WITH FORMAT, INIT, NAME = N'Log Backup', STATS = 10;
```

---

## 🔁 Step 3 — Restore on Mirror Server

Restore on the **Mirror Server** using `NORECOVERY` — the database must remain in a **restoring state**.

```sql
-- Restore Full Backup
RESTORE DATABASE [YourDatabaseName]
FROM DISK = N'C:\Backups\YourDatabaseName_FULL.bak'
WITH NORECOVERY, STATS = 10;

-- Restore Log Backup
RESTORE LOG [YourDatabaseName]
FROM DISK = N'C:\Backups\YourDatabaseName_LOG.bak'
WITH NORECOVERY, STATS = 10;
```

> ✅ The mirror database should show as **"Restoring..."** in SSMS — this is correct!

